## an5.34
an5.34:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Vesālī in the hall with the peaked roof in the Great Wood.
an5.34:1.2 | S: Then General Sīha went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and asked him, | T: Then Sīha the general approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said:
an5.34:1.3 | S: “Sir, can you point out a fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life?” | T: “Is it possible, Bhante, to point out a directly visible fruit of giving?”
an5.34:2.1 | S: “I can, Sīha,” said the Buddha. | T: “It is, Sīha,” the Blessed One said.
an5.34:2.2 | S: “A giver, a donor is dear and beloved to many people. | T: (1) “A donor, Sīha, a munificent giver, is dear and agreeable to many people.
an5.34:2.3 | S: This is a fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: This is a directly visible fruit of giving.
an5.34:3.1 | S: Furthermore, good and true persons associate with a giver. | T: (2) “Again, good persons resort to a donor, a munificent giver.
an5.34:3.2 | S: This is another fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: This, too, is a directly visible fruit of giving.
an5.34:4.1 | S: Furthermore, a giver gains a good reputation. | T: (3) “Again, a donor, a munificent giver, acquires a good reputation.
an5.34:4.2 | S: This is another fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: This, too, is a directly visible fruit of giving.
an5.34:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a giver enters any kind of assembly bold and self-assured, whether it’s an assembly of aristocrats, brahmins, householders, or ascetics. | T: (4) “Again, whatever assembly a donor, a munificent giver, approaches—whether of khattiyas, brahmins, householders, or ascetics—he approaches it confidently and composed.
an5.34:5.2 | S: This is another fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: This too is a directly visible fruit of giving.
an5.34:6.1 | S: Furthermore, when a giver’s body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm. | T: (5) “Again, with the breakup of the body, after death, a donor, a munificent giver, is reborn in a good destination, in a heavenly world.
an5.34:6.2 | S: This is a fruit of giving to do with lives to come.” | T: This is a fruit of giving pertaining to future lives.”
an5.34:7.1 | S: When he said this, General Sīha said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Sīha the general said to the Blessed One:
an5.34:7.2 | S: “When it comes to those four fruits of giving that are apparent in the present life, I don’t rely on faith in the Buddha, for I know them too. | T: “Bhante, I do not go by faith in the Blessed One concerning those four directly visible fruits of giving declared by him. I know them, too.
an5.34:7.3 | S: I’m a giver, a donor, and am dear and beloved to many people. | T: For I am a donor, a munificent giver, and I am dear and agreeable to many people.
an5.34:7.4 | S: I’m a giver, and good and true persons associate with me. | T: I am a donor, a munificent giver, and many good persons resort to me.
an5.34:7.5 | S: I’m a giver, and I have this good reputation: ‘General Sīha gives, serves, and attends on the Saṅgha.’ | T: I am a donor, a munificent giver, and I have acquired a good reputation as a donor, sponsor, and supporter of the Saṅgha.
an5.34:7.6 | S: I’m a giver, and I enter any kind of assembly bold and self-assured, whether it’s an assembly of aristocrats, brahmins, householders, or ascetics. | T: I am a donor, a munificent giver, and whatever assembly I approach—whether of khattiyas, brahmins, householders, or ascetics—I approach it confidently and composed.
an5.34:7.7 | S: When it comes to these four fruits of giving that are apparent in the present life, I don’t rely on faith in the Buddha, for I know them too. | T: I do not go by faith in the Blessed One concerning these four directly visible fruits of giving declared by him. I know them, too.
an5.34:7.8 | S: But when the Buddha says: | T: But when the Blessed One tells me:
an5.34:7.9 | S: ‘When a giver’s body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm.’ I don’t know this, so I have to rely on faith in the Buddha.” | T: ‘Sīha, with the breakup of the body, after death, a donor, a munificent giver, is reborn in a good destination, in a heavenly world,’ I do not know this, and here I go by faith in the Blessed One.”
an5.34:7.10 | S: “That’s so true, Sīha! That’s so true! | T: “So it is, Sīha, so it is!
an5.34:7.11 | S: When a giver’s body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm. | T: With the breakup of the body, after death, a donor, a munificent giver, is reborn in a good destination, in a heavenly world.”
an5.34:8.1 | S: Giving, you’re loved and befriended by many people. | T: By giving, he becomes dear and many resort to him.
an5.34:8.2 | S: You get a good reputation, and your fame grows. | T: He attains a good reputation and his fame increases.
an5.34:8.3 | S: A generous man enters an assembly | T: The generous man is composed and confidently enters the assembly.
an5.34:8.4 | S: bold and self-assured. | T: (none)
an5.34:9.1 | S: So an astute person, seeking happiness, <j>would give gifts, | T: Therefore, seeking happiness, wise persons give gifts,
an5.34:9.2 | S: having driven out the stain of stinginess. | T: having removed the stain of miserliness.
an5.34:9.3 | S: They live long in the Third Heaven, | T: When they are settled in the triple heaven, for a long time they delight
an5.34:9.4 | S: enjoying the company of the gods. | T: in companionship with the devas.
an5.34:10.1 | S: Having taken the opportunity to do good, <j>when they pass from here | T: Having taken the opportunity to do wholesome deeds, passing from here,
an5.34:10.2 | S: they wander self-radiant in the Garden of Delight. | T: self-luminous, they roam in Nandana,
an5.34:10.3 | S: There they delight, rejoice, and enjoy themselves, | T: where they delight, rejoice, and enjoy themselves,
an5.34:10.4 | S: provided with the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: furnished with the five objects of sensual pleasure.
an5.34:10.5 | S: Having practiced the word of the unattached, <j>the unaffected, | T: Having fulfilled the word of the unattached Stable One,
an5.34:10.6 | S: disciples of the Holy One rejoice in heaven.” | T: the Fortunate One’s disciples rejoice in heaven.
an5.34:10.7 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn16.3
sn16.3:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn16.3:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, you should approach families like the moon: | T: “Bhikkhus, you should approach families like the moon—
sn16.3:1.3 | S: withdrawn in body and mind, ever the newcomer, and never rude. | T: drawing back the body and mind, always acting like newcomers, without impudence towards families.
sn16.3:1.4 | S: Suppose a person were to look down at an old well, a rugged cliff, or an inaccessible riverland. | T: Just as a man looking down an old well, a precipice, or a steep riverbank
sn16.3:1.5 | S: They’d withdraw their body and mind. | T: would draw back the body and mind,
sn16.3:1.6 | S: In the same way, you should approach families like the moon: | T: so too, bhikkhus, should you approach families.
sn16.3:1.7 | S: withdrawn in body and mind, always the newcomer, and never rude. | T: (none)
sn16.3:2.1 | S: Kassapa approaches families like the moon: | T: “Bhikkhus, Kassapa approaches families like the moon—
sn16.3:2.2 | S: withdrawn in body and mind, ever the newcomer, and never rude. | T: drawing back the body and mind, always acting like a newcomer, without impudence towards families.
sn16.3:2.3 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: What do you think, bhikkhus,
sn16.3:2.4 | S: What kind of mendicant is worthy of approaching families?” | T: what kind of bhikkhu is worthy to approach families?”
sn16.3:2.5 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: “Venerable sir, our teachings are rooted in the Blessed One, guided by the Blessed One, take recourse in the Blessed One. It would be good if the Blessed One would clear up the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from him, the bhikkhus will remember it.”
sn16.3:3.1 | S: Then the Buddha waved his hand in space. | T: Then the Blessed One waved his hand in space and said:
sn16.3:3.2 | S: “Mendicants, this hand is not stuck or held or caught in space. | T: “Bhikkhus, just as this hand does not get caught in space, is not held fast by it, is not bound by it,
sn16.3:3.3 | S: In the same way, when approaching families, a mendicant’s mind is not stuck or held or caught, thinking: | T: so when a bhikkhu approaches families his mind does not get caught, held fast, and bound amidst families, thinking:
sn16.3:3.4 | S: ‘May those who want material things get them, and may those who want merit make merits!’ | T: ‘May those desiring gains acquire gains, may those desiring merits make merits!’
sn16.3:3.5 | S: They’re just as pleased and happy when others get something as they are when they get something. | T: He is as elated and happy over the gains of others as he is over his own gains.
sn16.3:3.6 | S: This kind of mendicant is worthy of approaching families. | T: Such a bhikkhu is worthy to approach families.
sn16.3:4.1 | S: When Kassapa approaches families, his mind is not stuck or held or caught, thinking: | T: “Bhikkhus, when Kassapa approaches families his mind does not get caught, held fast, or bound amidst families, thinking:

