 succeeded in 0ms:
sn16.3:4.2 | S: ‘May those who want material things get them, and may those who want merit make merits!’ | T: ‘May those desiring gains acquire gains, may those desiring merits make merits!’
sn16.3:4.3 | S: He’s just as pleased and happy when others get something as he is when he gets something. | T: He is as elated and happy over the gains of others as he is over his own gains.
sn16.3:4.4 | P: evaṁ paresaṁ lābhena attamano hoti sumano. | T: (none)
sn16.3:5.1 | S: What do you think, mendicants? | T: “What do you think, bhikkhus,
sn16.3:5.2 | S: What kind of mendicant’s teaching is pure, and what kind is impure?” | T: how is a bhikkhu’s teaching of the Dhamma impure, and how is his teaching of the Dhamma pure?”
sn16.3:5.3 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. He is our guide and our refuge. Sir, may the Buddha himself please clarify the meaning of this. The mendicants will listen and remember it.” | T: “Venerable sir, our teachings are rooted in the Blessed One….”
sn16.3:5.4 | S: “Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then listen and attend closely, bhikkhus, I will speak.”
sn16.3:5.5 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” those bhikkhus replied.
sn16.3:5.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn16.3:6.1 | S: “Whoever teaches Dhamma to others with the thought: | T: “A bhikkhu teaches the Dhamma to others with the thought:
sn16.3:6.2 | S: ‘Oh! May they listen to the teaching from me. When they’ve heard it, may they gain confidence in the teaching and demonstrate their confidence to me.’ | T: ‘Oh, may they listen to the Dhamma from me! Having listened, may they gain confidence in the Dhamma! Being confident, may they show their confidence to me!’
sn16.3:6.3 | S: Such a mendicant’s teaching is impure. | T: Such a bhikkhu’s teaching of the Dhamma is impure.
sn16.3:7.1 | S: Whoever teaches Dhamma to others with the thought: | T: “But a bhikkhu teaches the Dhamma to others with the thought:
sn16.3:7.2 | S: ‘The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves. | T: ‘The Dhamma is well expounded by the Blessed One, directly visible, immediate, inviting one to come and see, applicable, to be personally experienced by the wise.
sn16.3:7.3 | S: Oh! May they listen to the teaching from me. When they’ve heard it, may they understand the teaching and practice accordingly.’ | T: Oh, may they listen to the Dhamma from me! Having listened, may they understand the Dhamma! Having understood, may they practise accordingly!’
sn16.3:7.4 | S: So they teach others because of the clear rightness of the teaching, out of compassion, kindness, and sympathy. | T: Thus he teaches the Dhamma to others because of the intrinsic excellence of the Dhamma; he teaches the Dhamma to others from compassion and sympathy, out of tender concern.
sn16.3:7.5 | S: Such a mendicant’s teaching is pure. | T: Such a bhikkhu’s teaching of the Dhamma is pure.
sn16.3:8.1 | S: Kassapa teaches Dhamma to others with the thought: | T: “Bhikkhus, Kassapa teaches the Dhamma to others with the thought:
sn16.3:8.2 | S: ‘The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves. | T: ‘The Dhamma is well expounded by the Blessed One….
sn16.3:8.3 | S: Oh! May they listen to the teaching from me. When they’ve heard it, may they understand the teaching and practice accordingly.’ | T: Oh, may they listen to the Dhamma from me! Having listened, may they understand the Dhamma! Having understood, may they practise accordingly!’
sn16.3:8.4 | S: Thus he teaches others because of the clear rightness of the teaching, out of compassion, kindness, and sympathy. | T: He teaches the Dhamma to others because of the intrinsic excellence of the Dhamma; he teaches the Dhamma to others from compassion and sympathy, out of tender concern.
sn16.3:8.5 | S: I will exhort you with the example of Kassapa or someone like him. You should practice accordingly.” | T: “Bhikkhus, I will exhort you by the example of Kassapa or one who is similar to Kassapa. Being exhorted, you should practise accordingly.”
sn16.3:8.6 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.32
an5.32:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrel sanctuary.
an5.32:1.2 | S: Then Princess Cundī, escorted by five hundred chariots and five hundred royal maidens, went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Princess Cundī, accompanied by five hundred chariots and five hundred court girls, approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and sat down to one side. Princess Cundī then said to the Blessed One:
an5.32:2.1 | S: “Sir, my brother, Prince Cunda, says this: | T: “Bhante, my brother is Prince Cunda. He says thus:
an5.32:2.2 | S: ‘Take a woman or man who goes for refuge to the Buddha, the teaching, and the Saṅgha, and doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, commit sexual misconduct, lie, or consume intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor. Only then do they get reborn in a good place, not a bad place, when their body breaks up, after death.’ | T: ‘Whenever a man or a woman has gone for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha, and abstains from the destruction of life, from taking what is not given, from sexual misconduct, from false speech, and from indulging in liquor, wine, and intoxicants, the basis for heedlessness, with the breakup of the body, after death, he is reborn only in a good destination, not in a bad destination.’
an5.32:2.3 | S: And so I ask the Buddha: | T: I ask the Blessed One:
an5.32:2.4 | S: Sir, what kind of teacher should you have confidence in so as to be reborn in a good place, not a bad place, when the body breaks up, after death? | T: ‘What kind of teacher, Bhante, should one have confidence in, so that, with the breakup of the body, after death, one is reborn only in a good destination, not in a bad destination?
an5.32:2.5 | S: Sir, what kind of teaching should you have confidence in so as to be reborn in a good place, not a bad place, when the body breaks up, after death? | T: What kind of Dhamma should one have confidence in, so that, with the breakup of the body, after death, one is reborn only in a good destination, not in a bad destination?
an5.32:2.6 | S: Sir, what kind of Saṅgha should you have confidence in so as to be reborn in a good place, not a bad place, when the body breaks up, after death? | T: What kind of Saṅgha should one have confidence in, so that, with the breakup of the body, after death, one is reborn only in a good destination, not in a bad destination?
an5.32:2.7 | S: Sir, what kind of ethics should you fulfill so as to be reborn in a good place, not a bad place, when the body breaks up, after death?” | T: What kind of virtuous behavior should one fulfill so that, with the breakup of the body, after death, one is reborn only in a good destination, not in a bad destination?”
an5.32:3.1 | S: “Cundī, the Realized One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha, is said to be the best of all sentient beings—be they footless, with two feet, four feet, or many feet; with form or formless; with perception or without perception or with neither perception nor non-perception. | T: (1) “Cundī, to whatever extent there are beings, whether footless or with two feet, four feet, or many feet, whether having form or formless, whether percipient, non-percipient, or neither percipient nor non-percipient, the Tathāgata, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One is declared the foremost among them.
an5.32:3.2 | S: Those who have confidence in the Buddha have confidence in the best. | T: Those who have confidence in the Buddha have confidence in the foremost,
an5.32:3.3 | S: Having confidence in the best, the result is the best. | T: and for those who have confidence in the foremost, the result is foremost.
an5.32:4.1 | S: The noble eightfold path is said to be the best of all conditioned things. | T: (2) “To whatever extent, Cundī, there are phenomena that are conditioned, the noble eightfold path is declared the foremost among them.
an5.32:4.2 | S: Those who have confidence in the noble eightfold path have confidence in the best. Having confidence in the best, the result is the best. | T: Those who have confidence in the noble eightfold path have confidence in the foremost, and for those who have confidence in the foremost, the result is foremost.
an5.32:5.1 | S: Fading away is said to be the best of all things whether conditioned or unconditioned. | T: (3) “To whatever extent, Cundī, there are phenomena whether conditioned or unconditioned, dispassion is declared the foremost among them,
an5.32:5.2 | S: That is, the quelling of vanity, the removing of thirst, the uprooting of clinging, the breaking of the round, the ending of craving, fading away, cessation, extinguishment. | T: that is, the crushing of pride, the removal of thirst, the uprooting of attachment, the termination of the round, the destruction of craving, dispassion, cessation, nibbāna.
an5.32:5.3 | S: Those who have confidence in the teaching of fading away have confidence in the best. | T: Those who have confidence in the Dhamma, in dispassion, have confidence in the foremost,
an5.32:5.4 | S: Having confidence in the best, the result is the best. | T: and for those who have confidence in the foremost, the result is foremost.
an5.32:6.1 | S: The Saṅgha of the Realized One’s disciples is said to be the best of all communities and groups. | T: (4) “To whatever extent, Cundī, there are Saṅghas or groups, the Saṅgha of the Tathāgata’s disciples is declared the foremost among them,
an5.32:6.2 | S: It consists of the four pairs, the eight individual persons. This is the Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples that is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of greeting with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world. | T: that is, the four pairs of persons, the eight types of individuals—this Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples is worthy of gifts, worthy of hospitality, worthy of offerings, worthy of reverential salutation, the unsurpassed field of merit for the world.
an5.32:6.3 | S: Those who have confidence in the Saṅgha have confidence in the best. | T: Those who have confidence in the Saṅgha have confidence in the foremost,
an5.32:6.4 | S: Having confidence in the best, the result is the best. | T: and for those who have confidence in the foremost, the result is foremost.
an5.32:7.1 | S: The ethical conduct loved by the noble ones is said to be the best of all ethics. | T: (5) “To whatever extent, Cundī, there is virtuous behavior, the virtuous behavior loved by the noble ones is declared the foremost among them,
an5.32:7.2 | S: It is intact, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred, liberating, praised by sensible people, not mistaken, and leading to immersion. | T: that is, when it is unbroken, flawless, unblemished, unblotched, freeing, praised by the wise, ungrasped, leading to concentration.
an5.32:7.3 | S: Those who fulfill the ethics loved by the noble ones fulfill the best. | T: Those who fulfill the virtuous behavior loved by the noble ones fulfill the foremost,
an5.32:7.4 | S: Fulfilling the best, the result is the best. | T: and for those who fulfill the foremost, the result is foremost.”
an5.32:8.1 | S: For those who, knowing the best teaching, | T: For those confident in regard to the foremost, knowing the foremost Dhamma,
an5.32:8.2 | S: base their confidence on the best— | T: (none)
an5.32:8.3 | S: confident in the best Awakened One, | T: confident in the Buddha—the foremost—
an5.32:8.4 | S: supremely worthy of a religious donation; | T: unsurpassed, worthy of offerings;
an5.32:9.1 | S: confident in the best teaching, | T: for those confident in the foremost Dhamma,
an5.32:9.2 | S: the bliss of fading and stilling; | T: in the blissful peace of dispassion;
an5.32:9.3 | S: confident in the best Saṅgha, | T: for those confident in the foremost Saṅgha,
an5.32:9.4 | S: the supreme field of merit— | T: the unsurpassed field of merit;
an5.32:10.1 | S: giving gifts to the best, | T: for those giving gifts to the foremost,
an5.32:10.2 | S: the best of merit grows: | T: the foremost kind of merit increases:
an5.32:10.3 | S: the best lifespan, beauty, | T: the foremost life span, beauty, and glory,
an5.32:10.4 | S: fame, reputation, happiness, and strength. | T: good reputation, happiness, and strength.
an5.32:11.1 | S: An intelligent person gives to the best, | T: The wise one who gives to the foremost,
an5.32:11.2 | S: settled on the best teaching. | T: concentrated upon the foremost Dhamma,
an5.32:11.3 | S: When they become a god or human, | T: having become a deva or human being,
an5.32:11.4 | S: they rejoice at reaching the best.” | T: rejoices having attained the foremost.
an5.32:11.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn36.11
sn36.11:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn36.11:1.2 | S: “Just now, sir, as I was in private retreat this thought came to mind. | T: “Here, venerable sir, while I was alone in seclusion, a reflection arose in my mind thus:
sn36.11:1.3 | S: The Buddha has spoken of three feelings. | T: ‘Three feeling have been spoken of by the Blessed One:
sn36.11:1.4 | S: Pleasant, painful, and neutral feeling. | T: pleasant feeling, painful feeling, neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling.
sn36.11:1.5 | S: These are the three feelings the Buddha has spoken of. | T: These three feelings have been spoken of by the Blessed One.
sn36.11:1.6 | S: But the Buddha has also said: | T: But the Blessed One has said:
sn36.11:1.7 | S: ‘Suffering includes whatever is felt.’ | T: “Whatever is felt is included in suffering.”
sn36.11:1.8 | S: What was the Buddha referring to when he said this?” | T: Now with reference to what was this stated by the Blessed One?’”
sn36.11:1.9 | P: ‘yaṁ kiñci vedayitaṁ taṁ dukkhasmin’”ti? | T: (none)
sn36.11:2.1 | S: “Good, good, mendicant! | T: “Good, good, bhikkhu!
sn36.11:2.2 | S: I have spoken of these three feelings. | T: These three feelings have been spoken of by me:
sn36.11:2.3 | S: Pleasant, painful, and neutral feeling. | T: pleasant feeling, painful feeling, neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling.
sn36.11:2.4 | S: These are the three feelings I have spoken of. | T: These three feelings have been spoken of by me.
sn36.11:2.5 | S: But I have also said: | T: And I have also said:
sn36.11:2.6 | S: ‘Suffering includes whatever is felt.’ | T: ‘Whatever is felt is included in suffering.’
sn36.11:2.7 | S: When I said this I was referring to the impermanence of conditions, to the fact that conditions are | T: That has been stated by me with reference to the impermanence of formations.
sn36.11:2.8 | P: ‘yaṁ kiñci vedayitaṁ taṁ dukkhasmin’ti. | T: (none)
sn36.11:2.9 | S: liable to end, | T: That has been stated by me with reference to formations being subject to destruction …
sn36.11:2.10 | S: vanish, | T: to formations being subject to vanishing …
sn36.11:2.11 | S: fade away, | T: to formations being subject to fading away …
sn36.11:2.12 | S: cease, | T: to formations being subject to cessation …
sn36.11:2.13 | S: and perish. | T: to formations being subject to change.
sn36.11:2.14 | P: ‘yaṁ kiñci vedayitaṁ taṁ dukkhasmin’ti. | T: (none)
sn36.11:2.15 | S: But I have also explained the progressive cessation of activities. | T: “Then, bhikkhu, I have also taught the successive cessation of formations.
sn36.11:2.16 | S: For someone who has attained the first absorption, speech has ceased. | T: For one who has attained the first jhana, speech has ceased.
sn36.11:2.17 | S: For someone who has attained the second absorption, the placing of the mind and keeping it connected have ceased. | T: For one who has attained the second jhana, thought and examination have ceased.
sn36.11:2.18 | S: For someone who has attained the third absorption, rapture has ceased. | T: For one who has attained the third jhana, rapture has ceased.
sn36.11:2.19 | S: For someone who has attained the fourth absorption, breathing has ceased. | T: For one who has attained the fourth jhana, in-breathing and out-breathing have ceased.
sn36.11:2.20 | S: For someone who has attained the dimension of infinite space, the perception of form has ceased. | T: For one who has attained the base of the infinity of space, the perception of form has ceased.
sn36.11:2.21 | S: For someone who has attained the dimension of infinite consciousness, the perception of the dimension of infinite space has ceased. | T: For one who has attained the base of the infinity of consciousness, the perception pertaining to the base of the infinity of space has ceased.
sn36.11:2.22 | S: For someone who has attained the dimension of nothingness, the perception of the dimension of infinite consciousness has ceased. | T: For one who has attained the base of nothingness, the perception pertaining to the base of the infinity of consciousness has ceased.
sn36.11:2.23 | S: For someone who has attained the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, the perception of the dimension of nothingness has ceased. | T: For one who has attained the base of neither-perception-nor-nonperception, the perception pertaining to the base of nothingness has ceased.
sn36.11:2.24 | S: For someone who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling, perception and feeling have ceased. | T: For one who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling, perception and feeling have ceased.
sn36.11:2.25 | S: For a mendicant who has ended the defilements, greed, hate, and delusion have ceased. | T: For a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed, lust has ceased, hatred has ceased, delusion has ceased.
sn36.11:2.26 | S: And I have also explained the progressive settling of activities. | T: “Then, bhikkhu, I have also taught the successive subsiding of formations.
sn36.11:2.27 | S: For someone who has attained the first absorption, speech has stilled. | T: For one who has attained the first jhana speech has subsided….
sn36.11:2.28 | S: For someone who has attained the second absorption, the placing of the mind and keeping it connected have stilled. … | T: (none)
sn36.11:2.29 | S: For someone who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling, perception and feeling have stilled. | T: For one who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling, perception and feeling have subsided.
sn36.11:2.30 | S: For a mendicant who has ended the defilements, greed, hate, and delusion have stilled. | T: For a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed, lust has subsided, hatred has subsided, delusion has subsided.
sn36.11:2.31 | S: There are these six levels of tranquility. | T: “There are, bhikkhu, these six kinds of tranquillization.
sn36.11:2.32 | S: For someone who has attained the first absorption, speech has been tranquilized. | T: For one who has attained the first jhana, speech has been tranquillized.
sn36.11:2.33 | S: For someone who has attained the second absorption, the placing of the mind and keeping it connected have been tranquilized. | T: For one who has attained the second jhana, thought and examination have been tranquillized.
sn36.11:2.34 | S: For someone who has attained the third absorption, rapture has been tranquilized. | T: For one who has attained the third jhana, rapture has been tranquillized.
sn36.11:2.35 | S: For someone who has attained the fourth absorption, breathing has been tranquilized. | T: For one who has attained the fourth jhana, in-breathing and out-breathing have been tranquillized.
sn36.11:2.36 | S: For someone who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling, perception and feeling have been tranquilized. | T: For one who has attained the cessation of perception and feeling, perception and feeling have been tranquillized.
sn36.11:2.37 | S: For a mendicant who has ended the defilements, greed, hate, and delusion have been tranquilized.” | T: For a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed, lust has been tranquillized, hatred has been tranquillized, delusion has been tranquillized.”
sn36.11:2.38 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


